  <problem_id>polymath_00911</problem_id>
  <dimension1_verdict>PARTIAL_PROGRESS</dimension1_verdict>
  <dimension1_explanation>The AI correctly derived the key formula m = n + 2p - 2 (via angle-sum/Euler's formula), correctly identified that p = 0 fails (so p ≥ 1 and thus m ≥ n), and even constructed valid configurations achieving m = n for specific cases (n = 5 by placing the auxiliary vertex near a polygon vertex; n = 6 by placing it at the center and connecting to every other vertex). However, it never found the standard solution's elegant general construction — triangulate into n-2 triangles with exactly one acute triangle, then split that acute triangle at its incenter into 3 obtuse triangles — nor did it find the clean general lower-bound argument (the circumcenter must lie inside one triangle, forcing it to be acute/right). The AI was stuck doing case-by-case analysis (n = 5, 6, 7) and never generalized.</dimension1_explanation>
  <dimension2_turning_point_type>other</dimension2_turning_type>
  <dimension2_explanation>The key turning point is geometric, not number-theoretic: (1) the lower bound uses the fact that without auxiliary vertices all triangles share the polygon's circumcircle, so the common circumcenter lies inside/on one triangle, making it acute or right — hence auxiliary vertices are necessary; (2) the upper bound uses the incenter of the single acute triangle in a standard triangulation, splitting it into 3 obtuse triangles. Neither technique fits the listed number-theory categories.</dimension2_explanation>
  <ai_direction_summary>The AI worked in the correct general framework (Euler/angle-sum formula, auxiliary-vertex counting, explicit constructions) but got bogged down in case-by-case verification for n = 5, 6, 7 without discovering the unifying circumcenter lower-bound argument or the incenter-splitting general construction.</ai_direction_summary>
  <standard_solution_key_technique>The standard solution proves m ≥ n via the shared-circumcircle/circumcenter argument (forcing one triangle to be non-obtuse without auxiliary vertices) and proves m = n is achievable by splitting the unique acute triangle of a standard triangulation at its incenter into 3 obtuse triangles.</standard_solution_key_technique>
